# Pulumi ComponentResource Migration with Aliases (No Replacement)

This guide shows how to refactor a flat Pulumi TypeScript program into ComponentResources while preserving existing cloud resources using `aliases`.

## Key Concept

When you refactor resources from flat to nested structure, Pulumi sees them as new resources with new URNs (Uniform Resource Names). To prevent destruction and recreation, declare `aliases` pointing to the original URNs.

---

## Before: Flat Structure

```typescript
// index.ts (flat program)
import * as aws from "@pulumi/aws";
import * as pulumi from "@pulumi/pulumi";

const config = new pulumi.Config();
const env = pulumi.getStack(); // "dev" or "prod"

// EKS Cluster
const cluster = new aws.eks.Cluster(`app-cluster`, {
  roleArn: clusterRole.arn,
  vpcConfig: { subnetIds: [...] },
  // ...
}, { protect: true });

// S3 Bucket
const bucket = new aws.s3.Bucket(`app-assets`, {
  versioning: { enabled: true },
  serverSideEncryptionConfiguration: { ... },
  // ...
});

// RDS Instance
const dbInstance = new aws.rds.Instance(`app-db`, {
  engine: "postgres",
  instanceClass: env === "prod" ? "db.t3.medium" : "db.t3.micro",
  // ...
});

// IAM Role + User
const serviceRole = new aws.iam.Role(`app-service-role`, { ... });

export const clusterName = cluster.name;
export const bucketName = bucket.id;
export const dbEndpoint = dbInstance.endpoint;
```

---

## After: ComponentResource Structure

### Step 1: Create a Component Class

```typescript
// components/appComponent.ts
import * as aws from "@pulumi/aws";
import * as pulumi from "@pulumi/pulumi";

export interface AppComponentArgs {
  env: string;
  subnetIds: pulumi.Input<string>[];
  vpcId: pulumi.Input<string>;
}

export class AppComponent extends pulumi.ComponentResource {
  public readonly cluster: aws.eks.Cluster;
  public readonly bucket: aws.s3.Bucket;
  public readonly dbInstance: aws.rds.Instance;
  public readonly serviceRole: aws.iam.Role;

  public readonly clusterName: pulumi.Output<string>;
  public readonly bucketName: pulumi.Output<string>;
  public readonly dbEndpoint: pulumi.Output<string>;

  constructor(
    name: string,
    args: AppComponentArgs,
    opts?: pulumi.ComponentResourceOptions
  ) {
    super("myapp:infrastructure:AppComponent", name, {}, opts);

    const defaultResourceOpts: pulumi.CustomResourceOptions = {
      parent: this,
    };

    // ===== EKS Cluster =====
    this.cluster = new aws.eks.Cluster(
      `${name}-cluster`,
      {
        roleArn: clusterRole.arn, // assume already created or passed in
        vpcConfig: {
          subnetIds: args.subnetIds,
        },
        version: "1.27",
        // ... other config
      },
      {
        ...defaultResourceOpts,
        // Alias pointing to the OLD flat resource name
        aliases: [{ name: "app-cluster" }],
        protect: true,
      }
    );

    // ===== S3 Bucket =====
    this.bucket = new aws.s3.Bucket(
      `${name}-assets`,
      {
        versioning: {
          enabled: true,
        },
        serverSideEncryptionConfiguration: {
          rule: {
            applyServerSideEncryptionByDefault: {
              sseAlgorithm: "AES256",
            },
          },
        },
        // ... other config
      },
      {
        ...defaultResourceOpts,
        aliases: [{ name: "app-assets" }],
      }
    );

    // ===== RDS Instance =====
    const dbInstanceClass =
      args.env === "prod" ? "db.t3.medium" : "db.t3.micro";

    this.dbInstance = new aws.rds.Instance(
      `${name}-db`,
      {
        engine: "postgres",
        instanceClass: dbInstanceClass,
        allocatedStorage: args.env === "prod" ? 100 : 20,
        multiAz: args.env === "prod",
        deletionProtection: true,
        backupRetentionPeriod: 7,
        publiclyAccessible: false,
        // ... other config
      },
      {
        ...defaultResourceOpts,
        aliases: [{ name: "app-db" }],
      }
    );

    // ===== IAM Role =====
    this.serviceRole = new aws.iam.Role(
      `${name}-service-role`,
      {
        assumeRolePolicy: pulumi.interpolate`{
          "Version": "2012-10-17",
          "Statement": [
            {
              "Action": "sts:AssumeRole",
              "Effect": "Allow",
              "Principal": {
                "Service": "eks.amazonaws.com"
              }
            }
          ]
        }`,
      },
      {
        ...defaultResourceOpts,
        aliases: [{ name: "app-service-role" }],
      }
    );

    // ===== Export outputs =====
    this.clusterName = this.cluster.name;
    this.bucketName = this.bucket.id;
    this.dbEndpoint = this.dbInstance.endpoint;

    this.registerOutputs({
      clusterName: this.clusterName,
      bucketName: this.bucketName,
      dbEndpoint: this.dbEndpoint,
    });
  }
}
```

### Step 2: Update Main Stack

```typescript
// index.ts (refactored)
import * as pulumi from "@pulumi/pulumi";
import * as aws from "@pulumi/aws";
import { AppComponent } from "./components/appComponent";

const config = new pulumi.Config();
const env = pulumi.getStack(); // "dev" or "prod"

// Assume VPC already exists or is created separately
const vpc = aws.ec2.getVpc({ default: true });
const subnets = aws.ec2.getSubnets({ filters: [...] });

const app = new AppComponent("app", {
  env,
  vpcId: vpc.then((v) => v.id),
  subnetIds: subnets.then((s) => s.ids),
});

export const clusterName = app.clusterName;
export const bucketName = app.bucketName;
export const dbEndpoint = app.dbEndpoint;
```

---

## Understanding Aliases

### Old URN (flat structure)
```
urn:pulumi:dev::myproject::aws:s3/bucket:Bucket::app-assets
```

### New URN (nested in component)
```
urn:pulumi:dev::myproject::myapp:infrastructure:AppComponent$aws:s3/bucket:Bucket::app-assets
```

### Alias Configuration

The `aliases` property tells Pulumi: *"This is the same resource; ignore the URI change."*

```typescript
aliases: [{ name: "app-assets" }]
```

This resolves to the old URN by inferring the parent stack and type. Alternatively, provide the full URN:

```typescript
aliases: [
  new pulumi.Alias({
    name: "app-assets",
    // Inferred from context, or:
    // urn: "urn:pulumi:dev::myproject::aws:s3/bucket:Bucket::app-assets",
  }),
]
```

---

## Migration Workflow

### 1. **Prepare the Migration**

```bash
# Ensure you have a clean state
pulumi stack export dev > backup-dev.json
```

### 2. **Update Code** (add ComponentResource)

Add the component class and update `index.ts` as shown above.

### 3. **Preview the Changes**

```bash
pulumi preview
```

**Expected output:**
```
  ~ aws:s3/bucket:Bucket: app-assets (has an alias)
      [no changes detected]
```

If Pulumi shows `delete` and `create`, **do not proceed**—check alias configuration.

### 4. **Apply the Migration**

```bash
pulumi up
```

Select `yes`. Pulumi updates the state file to map old URNs to new component-nested URNs via aliases. **No cloud resources are modified.**

### 5. **Verify**

```bash
pulumi stack export dev | grep '"urn"' | head -20
```

All resources should now have the component prefix in their URN:

```json
"urn": "urn:pulumi:dev::myproject::myapp:infrastructure:AppComponent$aws:s3/bucket:Bucket::app-assets"
```

---

## Handling Aliases Correctly

### ✅ Correct: Simple Name Alias

```typescript
aliases: [{ name: "app-assets" }]
```
Pulumi infers the old URN from the resource type and parent stack.

### ✅ Correct: Full URN Alias

```typescript
aliases: [
  new pulumi.Alias({
    urn: "urn:pulumi:dev::myproject::aws:s3/bucket:Bucket::app-assets",
  }),
]
```

### ✅ Correct: Parent + Name (for cross-component moves)

```typescript
aliases: [
  {
    parent: pulumi.rootStackResource,
    name: "app-assets",
  },
]
```

### ❌ Incorrect: Missing Alias

```typescript
new aws.s3.Bucket(`${name}-assets`, { ... }) // No aliases! → replacement triggered
```

### ❌ Incorrect: Wrong Name

```typescript
aliases: [{ name: "old-bucket-name" }] // Must match original resource name
```

---

## Multi-Environment (dev/prod) Considerations

Since the stack name changes (`dev` vs `prod`), the old URN is environment-specific. The alias mechanism handles this automatically:

```typescript
// dev.yaml stack
// URN: urn:pulumi:dev::myproject::aws:s3/bucket:Bucket::app-assets

// prod.yaml stack
// URN: urn:pulumi:prod::myproject::aws:s3/bucket:Bucket::app-assets
```

Each stack's state file maintains its own URN→alias mapping. Deploy to `dev` first, then `prod`:

```bash
pulumi stack select dev
pulumi up

pulumi stack select prod
pulumi up
```

---

## Troubleshooting

| Symptom | Cause | Fix |
|---------|-------|-----|
| `ResourceNotFound` or `NoSuchBucket` | Alias doesn't match old resource name | Check old stack export for exact name |
| Preview shows `delete` + `create` | Alias missing or incorrect | Re-check alias configuration |
| State file bloats after migration | Both old and new URN stored temporarily | This is normal; clean up in next `pulumi refresh` |
| Deployment hangs on `delete` | Resource has `protect: true` | Remove or update protection policy first |

---

## Testing Alias Correctness

Export the old state before migration:

```bash
pulumi stack export dev --show-secrets > old-state.json
```

After migration:

```bash
pulumi stack export dev > new-state.json
diff old-state.json new-state.json | grep -E '(urn|aliases)'
```

Verify:
1. All old URNs are now prefixed with the component type
2. `aliases` field now contains the old URN or name reference
3. Physical resource IDs (e.g., S3 bucket ARN, RDS endpoint) **remain unchanged**

```bash
# Verify no cloud resource changed
aws s3api head-bucket --bucket <bucket-name>  # Should succeed, same object
aws rds describe-db-instances --db-instance-identifier <id> | grep DBInstanceIdentifier
```

---

## Summary

| Step | Action |
|------|--------|
| 1 | Create `ComponentResource` class wrapping original resources |
| 2 | Add `aliases: [{ name: "old-resource-name" }]` to each resource |
| 3 | Update main `index.ts` to instantiate the component |
| 4 | Run `pulumi preview` and confirm no `delete`/`create` operations |
| 5 | Run `pulumi up` to update state file and URN mappings |
| 6 | Verify cloud resources are unchanged by querying AWS directly |

After migration, you have a maintainable component-based structure while preserving all cloud infrastructure—**zero downtime, zero resource replacement**.
